WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.920
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Derek.

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.083
DEREK DOWNEY: Hi, Martin.

00:00:03.083 --> 00:00:04.500
MARTIN OMANDER:
So, Derek, you are

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.150
a developer advocate
specializing in databases,

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:07.650
right?

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.025
DEREK DOWNEY:
Yeah, that's right.

00:00:09.025 --> 00:00:09.942
MARTIN OMANDER: Great.

00:00:09.942 --> 00:00:11.520
I have a question
from a customer who

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.220
is building the product on
top of Google Cloud using

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.310
serverless products and they
need a relational database

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:18.803
for their application.

00:00:18.803 --> 00:00:20.220
DEREK DOWNEY: Sure,
happy to help.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.601
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:29.818 --> 00:00:31.860
MARTIN OMANDER: The customer
is building a system

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.300
using Cloud Run, and
they picked Cloud Run

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.200
so they don't have to
provision and maintain servers.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.030
And this reduces
the ops work needed

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.560
to keep the system running.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.500
Initially, they picked
a NoSQL database

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.558
that helped them get their MVP
out the door really quickly.

00:00:46.558 --> 00:00:47.850
DEREK DOWNEY: That makes sense.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.100
MARTIN OMANDER: They got
great feedback from customers

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.170
for version 1 of their
system and they're

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.210
getting ready for version 2.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.450
They realized they will need
ACID transactions and also

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.120
aggregations, like
sums and averages,

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:03.120
across thousands of records
from their database.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.170
And they can't do that easily
in their NoSQL database.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:08.160
DEREK DOWNEY: Got it.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.320
Any particular issues
they're trying to solve

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.023
with ACID transactions?

00:01:12.023 --> 00:01:13.440
MARTIN OMANDER:
Yeah, they've been

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.470
having consistency issues with
the data as more customers use

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:17.070
their app.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:20.288
And they're spending a lot
of time fixing those issues.

00:01:20.288 --> 00:01:21.330
DEREK DOWNEY: Ah-ha, Yes.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:25.080
OK, do they have any
other requirements?

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.870
MARTIN OMANDER: Well, they
want a managed database

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.460
that scales as they grow.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.830
And they want to minimize
the ops work needed

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.135
to keep the system running.

00:01:34.135 --> 00:01:35.510
DEREK DOWNEY:
Well, in that case,

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.480
they should look
at Cloud Spanner.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.970
It's a fully-managed
relational database

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:43.510
that ensures strong consistency
and great availability.

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:45.680
And it has unlimited
scalability,

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.760
so it will easily scale
as their needs grow.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.220
MARTIN OMANDER: All right,
Derek, let's break that down.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.620
You started out
saying fully managed.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:55.360
What kind of ops
work would they need

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.575
to do to keep their
Spanner running smoothly?

00:01:58.575 --> 00:01:59.950
DEREK DOWNEY:
Well, for starters,

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.690
they don't have to
worry about patching

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:03.910
or other typical
server maintenance.

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:07.240
They also don't have to manage
any complex architectures

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:10.000
for load balancing or failovers.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.130
They don't even have
to manage encryption,

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.680
though Spanner has the
option for the customers

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.560
to manage encryption
keys if they require it.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.630
They will have to
manage access control

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:21.760
and schedule their own backups.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.190
They're also in charge
of schema migrations,

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:27.730
but Spanner manages the
deployment of those changes.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:30.520
Schema changes in Spanner
are online, though,

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:34.648
so they won't block the
app during the migration.

00:02:34.648 --> 00:02:35.440
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.620
And, next, you said
unlimited scalability.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.588
That's a pretty
bold claim, Derek.

00:02:40.588 --> 00:02:41.380
DEREK DOWNEY: Yeah.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:43.720
Spanner provides
consistent transactions,

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:48.340
like other relational databases,
and is a horizontally scaling

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:51.170
database similar
to NoSQL databases.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.560
So it grows with your data.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.600
To give you an idea,
Spanner processes

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.060
over 1 billion requests
per second at peak.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.150
MARTIN OMANDER:
Wow, that's a lot.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:03.580
Then you said
strong consistency.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:06.130
How can a global
relational database

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:08.020
be strongly consistent?

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.250
DEREK DOWNEY: Great question.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:12.910
Spanner relies on TrueTime
to provide commit timestamps

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:15.520
to guarantee global consistency.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:17.950
TrueTime is an
internal Google service

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:21.130
that guarantees monotonically
increasing timestamps

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:24.850
with microsecond granularity
across all nodes.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:27.160
These timestamps,
combined with Google's

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.560
dedicated infrastructure,
allows Spanner

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.263
to be strongly consistent
at low latencies.

00:03:33.263 --> 00:03:34.180
MARTIN OMANDER: Great.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:37.150
And, finally, you said
great availability.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:40.300
What does that mean
in this context?

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.520
DEREK DOWNEY: Spanner
comes with an SLA guarantee

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.650
of up to five nines
of availability

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.740
to ensure users have
access to their data.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:51.250
Spanner stores multiple copies
of your data in different zones

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:54.220
or regions to handle
maintenance and outages.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.480
This is transparent
to your application,

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:59.837
so your code doesn't need to
know where the replicas are.

00:03:59.837 --> 00:04:01.420
MARTIN OMANDER: It
sounds like Spanner

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:03.370
fits the needs of the customer.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:04.750
Could we take it for a spin?

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:05.710
DEREK DOWNEY: Yeah.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:07.810
What did you have in mind?

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:10.000
MARTIN OMANDER: My test
app is a simple REST

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.860
API for managing users.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:16.792
I'm thinking I'll swap out its
NoSQL database with Spanner

00:04:16.792 --> 00:04:18.459
to get a feel for how
much work it would

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:21.339
take to migrate a real project.

00:04:21.339 --> 00:04:23.230
DEREK DOWNEY: We
can totally do that.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:25.720
First, go to the Cloud Console.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:28.030
Click the hamburger
menu and scroll down

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:32.550
to the Databases section
and click Cloud Spanner.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.000
Then click Create Instance.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:35.940
MARTIN OMANDER: Mhm.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.410
OK, I did.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:37.910
Aha.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:39.840
I see a lot of options here.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:40.740
What should I pick?

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.320
DEREK DOWNEY: If all of your
users are in a single region,

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.990
you'd want a single
region configuration

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:47.760
to minimize latency.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.790
If the database will be
accessed from multiple regions

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.820
or you need five nines of
availability guarantees,

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:57.800
you'd want the
multiregion configuration.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.410
MARTIN OMANDER: Let's use
a regional configuration.

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:03.020
And I see that the
price changes up here

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.940
as I change the options.

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:06.680
Do I need a full node?

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.360
DEREK DOWNEY: Probably not yet.

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:11.117
To get started, you'd
probably want to go smaller.

00:05:11.117 --> 00:05:12.200
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.360
For now, I'll set
up a dev environment

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.210
with 100 processing
units, which will come out

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:21.260
to $0.09 per hour or
about $65 per month.

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:22.280
DEREK DOWNEY: Good idea.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:24.200
They can always scale
that instance up later

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:25.380
without any downtime.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.970
MARTIN OMANDER: All right,
I'll click Create here.

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:32.858
Now I assume my code will need
to change to access Spanner.

00:05:32.858 --> 00:05:33.650
DEREK DOWNEY: Yeah.

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:35.450
I sent you some code before.

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:36.440
Did you get it?

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.792
MARTIN OMANDER: Yep,
I'm pasting it in now.

00:05:39.792 --> 00:05:41.750
DEREK DOWNEY: Spanner
provides client libraries

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:44.270
for many languages,
including Node.js.

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:47.120
Since our application is
Node, all we have to do

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.540
is require the library,
instantiate the Spanner class,

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:54.270
and then provide the instance
and database information.

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:56.710
We could tune how session
pooling is handled,

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:59.340
but the defaults are a
great starting point.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:02.195
Accessing the data is
done with standard SQL.

00:06:02.195 --> 00:06:03.570
MARTIN OMANDER:
Does my code need

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:08.340
to specify which Spanner
replica or region to hit?

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:10.930
DEREK DOWNEY: No, Spanner
handles that for you.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:14.070
All you need to specify is
the instance and database.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:16.950
If Cloud Run is configured
with a service account that

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:19.050
can work with Spanner,
you don't even

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:20.790
have to provide credentials.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.190
Otherwise, you can
provide those, too.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:27.150
MARTIN OMANDER: All right, I'm
deploying my updated Cloud Run

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:28.080
service now.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:33.190
Looks like the API
is still working.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:36.153
So what kind of
loads can it handle?

00:06:36.153 --> 00:06:37.570
DEREK DOWNEY: I
set up a load test

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:39.970
that hits our test
application at roughly

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:43.210
3-to-1 reads to write,
which is the observed

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:44.920
pattern from production.

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:47.712
I'll start with 32
concurrent users.

00:06:47.712 --> 00:06:48.670
Let's see what happens.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.960
MARTIN OMANDER: Looks
like a decent amount

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.100
of operations per second here.

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:56.110
DEREK DOWNEY: Yes.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:59.537
Now let's look at the CPU
to see how it's holding up.

00:06:59.537 --> 00:07:00.370
MARTIN OMANDER: Mhm.

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:03.580
It looks like a load is
crossing the recommended CPU

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:04.810
threshold here.

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:06.457
What do we do?

00:07:06.457 --> 00:07:07.290
DEREK DOWNEY: Right.

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:09.540
If it goes much
beyond that threshold,

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:11.940
you would see request
latencies increase.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:13.540
We don't want that.

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:15.820
We can just increase
the processing units.

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:17.230
Let's do that now.

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:19.020
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:22.020
It looks like the CPU is
having an easier time now.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:25.650
And in Locust, we're able
to handle many more requests

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:27.450
per second than before.

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:29.520
Those extra units really helped.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:32.670
Pretty impressive for a globally
consistent relational database

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.310
with ACID transactions.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:37.800
Nice that you can tweak
capacity while the database is

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:39.480
under load.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:43.560
Yeah, and there are no changes
to the application needed.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:44.520
Great.

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:46.890
The customer will
get ACID transactions

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:48.900
in a managed database
with Spanner.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:51.270
They can start
small and scale up

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:53.790
without users being impacted
Thanks for showing me

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:55.128
how to do this, Derek.

00:07:55.128 --> 00:07:56.670
DEREK DOWNEY: You're
welcome, Martin.

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:58.837
I think Spanner would work
well for the next version

00:07:58.837 --> 00:08:00.275
of the customer's system.

00:08:00.275 --> 00:08:01.650
MARTIN OMANDER:
Yeah, I think so.

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:03.400
Thank you, everyone,
for watching.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:05.820
If you have questions for
me or Derek about Spanner,

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:07.870
please enter them in
the comments below.

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:10.290
Also let us know in the
comments if you have suggestions

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:13.320
for other serverless topics we
should bring up on the show.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:14.430
Bye for now.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:17.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

